                       Welcome to Moria!

Your first task is to generate a character. You must specify a sex,
a race and a class. Anytime before you have made these specifications,
you can interrupt with control-C. You can also specify the race as
often as you like by entering '%' to the class request. This will also
regenerate your attributes.

If this is your first time playing, you may want to read the manual first.

Moria Supreme keeps every Pmoria race and class, puts the Gnome back
beside the Spectre, and adds the Dryad. New professions are Druid,
Bard, and Adventurer.

Gnomes are small, quick, and clever. They make fine mages and rogues,
and poor front-line fighters. Dryads are wood spirits: high charisma
and wisdom, modest strength. They tend groves, not armies, and cannot
be warriors, paladins, or monks.

A Druid prays, favors healing and the life of the wild, and will not
wear metal armor. A Bard learns mage spells as songs, charm and lore
before fire, and does best in light armor. An Adventurer is a bit of
everything and master of none, and needs more experience to advance.

The goal is unchanged: one town, then down to the Balrog on level 50.

The town has an adventurers' hall (7), a bank (8), and a notice
board (?). The hall will identify one item, tell a rumor, or replace
a lost starting book. The bank holds gold for a fee and changes trade
bars. The board posts two small jobs. A rogue, ninja, bard, or jester
can find a guild door that looks like stone. In the temple, a priest,
paladin, druid, or healer may dedicate to one patron with D.
